    <name>
      <text>Integration by U substitution-q1</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate.</p>
<p>\( \displaystyle \int{@f_x@} dx\)</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked solution</b></p>
<p>To compute the definite integral for this integral, we can use the substitution method as follows:</p>
<p>Let \( \displaystyle u = {@bb@}\), \( \displaystyle \frac{du}{dx}={@diff_fx@}\), then \( \displaystyle dx={@du/diff_fx@}\)</p>
<p>Substituting the above expressions \(u\) and \(dx\) in the original integral we have:</p>
<p>
\(\begin{align} 
\displaystyle \int{@f_x@}dx &= \displaystyle \int {@x^aa@}.u.{@du/diff_fx@}\\
& = \displaystyle \int {@var_xx@}du\\
&= {@var_x1@} \\
\end{align}\)
</p>
<p>But \(u={@bb@}\), therefore, we need to replace it back in \(\left( {@var_x1@} \right)\) to find the final answer as shown:</p>
<p> \( \displaystyle \int{@f_x@}dx={@ta_1@} \)</p>
<p>where \(c\) is a constant of integration.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
